                <pb facs="0495" n="488" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg488"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/9707: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Grew</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-08-27T18:00:00-04:00">August 27, 1937—6
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>173. Your 317, August 26, 6 p.m. Consul at Tsingtao reports that evacuation of
                    Japanese nationals, excluding consular staff and a few men in important service,
                    has been ordered by the Japanese Government. It is stated that the order for
                    evacuation is based on “desire to avoid any possibility of a Sino-Japanese clash
                    in Tsingtao” and that evacuation will be completed within 5 days.</p>
                <p>The Department suggests that you might ask Foreign Office for confirmation of the
                    foregoing and at the same time take occasion to urge upon the Japanese
                    Government an early and favorable reply to note mentioned in your 297, August
                    23, 1 p.m., and Department’s previous.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
